bed in in three ways and these  intersect and overlap with each other to  some degree what is right what is  upright is another way of translating it  rectum what is honorable or noble a  little bit difficult to translate this  whole nessam the word that we get honest  from you might think of it as that which  is intrinsically valuable or that which  has its own goodness by its very nature  and then he brings up this other term  with virtue converter to thing which of  course could include virtue itself as  something that is good amma Stoics  thought that you know what is  fundamentally good has to do with the  you might say interior of the person and  the choices and the actions that they in  ajun so I think it's fairly easy to wrap  our heads around the comb vitter to tape  art you know with virtue  the Stoics recognized four main virtues  very similar to what the plate inist had  that is prudence justice temperance and  courage these are good states of  character that have been established in  a person that are displayed through  behavior consistently that you know are  durable they can be expected to go from  situation to situation and then the  actions that come out of that actions  that are guided by prudence that are  expressive of virtue that have their  route encouraged and and so on when it  comes to what is right and what is  honorable or noble I think it's very  often useful to look at examples that  Cicero will we'll use some of these are  examples of people showing the virtues  like courage she talks about a lot of  people in Roman history displaying  courage in their battles you know quite  desperate battles of Rome with its  enemies but also thriftiness and you  know moderation or temperance or justice  all of those those sorts of things and  in given situations the Stoics think  that we can in fact determine what would  be the right thing we're not going to  worry about you know what the exact way  of figuring that out would be and that  there are some things that are elevated  above others that we ought to prefer and  sacrifice for if need be the honorable  or Noble now what are not actually good  according to the Stoics this is where  people are more likely to diverge from  it so money for example money is not a  good it's not a bad thing as a matter of  fact the Stoics would talk about this in  a very technical way as they preferred  indifferent we're not going to worry  about that at this point but it is  something that it's okay to have houses  he talks about and we might think of you  know whatever domiciles furnishings that  you have for your house as he talks  about Corinthian vases  an example of the things that the Romans  had brought back with them as spoils for  war power offices positions certain  types of Honor we might think of in this  way not the Honorable as such but the  kind of honor that people give like  celebrity right having lots of likes on  your videos or your page or your Twitter  you know feed or whatever and many talks  later on about another thing that makes  its claim on us because some people say  yeah those those those things that you  can you know have or have taken away  from you that you can take away from  somebody else not really good but what  really is good is pleasure pleasure is  something that is more distinctively  your own and Cicero will say no that's  not a good either  the Stoics but that pleasure itself was  not a good in this they were going  against the grain not only of the  Epicureans who are another major  philosophical school at the time and the  common culture but also the Aristotelian  and some of the other schools of  philosophy now it gets very interesting  when we look at his his crew the answers  to the question why why aren't these  things good as opposed to this so Cicero  tells us that what is good should not in  some way turn out to be bad for the  person but we can think of you know all  sorts of cases where money didn't  actually make somebody'